# H13 query 5 — "Who/what actor proposed, approved, and executed it?"
# `executedBy` is populated starting Phase 6 (services/common/action_rdf.py::
# write_execution_finalized) — absent (unbound) for any decision that was
# never executed, which is itself an honest, queryable fact (not a guess).
#
# Phase 9 step 0b: scoped to GRAPH <%%GRAPH_IRI%%>. This is the query the
# orchestrator's Phase 8 review flagged directly: ?proposer/?principal/
# ?approver/?executor are all shared HumanActor/SoftwareAgent nodes
# re-asserted into EVERY decision's own named graph (same root cause as
# q9's Phase 7b fix), so the unscoped joins here fan out N times per shared
# node — once per OTHER decision that actor ever appeared in — and
# multiply again across the FOUR separate actor joins (proposer, principal,
# approver, executor) before DISTINCT can collapse them, i.e. up to N^4
# intermediate rows. Confirmed as the cause of
# test_forensic_query_5_traces_delegation_and_approval's httpx.ReadTimeout
# once the corpus reached ~5k decisions. Scoping the whole block to this
# decision's own graph makes every one of those joins resolve to exactly
# the one re-asserted copy that lives there.
PREFIX oo: <https://example.local/oo/>
SELECT DISTINCT ?proposerId ?principalOf ?approvedById ?executedById
WHERE {
  GRAPH <%%GRAPH_IRI%%> {
    ?decision oo:decisionId "%%DECISION_ID%%" ;
              oo:actor ?proposer .
    ?proposer oo:actorId ?proposerId .
    OPTIONAL { ?proposer oo:actsOnBehalfOf ?principal . ?principal oo:actorId ?principalOf . }
    OPTIONAL { ?decision oo:approvedBy ?approver . ?approver oo:actorId ?approvedById . }
    OPTIONAL { ?decision oo:executedBy ?executor . ?executor oo:actorId ?executedById . }
  }
}
